instruction:
Work in the Go repo at /gin (module github.com/gin-gonic/gin). TASK: fix a bug in /gin/context.go, function `NegotiateFormat` (content negotiation).

Current code (around line 1119):
```go
func (c *Context) NegotiateFormat(offered ...string) string {
	assert1(len(offered) > 0, "you must provide at least one offer")

	if c.Accepted == nil {
		c.Accepted = parseAccept(c.requestHeader("Accept"))
	}
	if len(c.Accepted) == 0 {
		return offered[0]
	}
	for _, accepted := range c.Accepted {
		for _, offer := range offered {
			// According to RFC 2616 and RFC 2396, non-ASCII characters are not allowed in headers,
			// therefore we can just iterate over the string without casting it into []rune
			i := 0
			for ; i < len(accepted); i++ {
				if accepted[i] == '*' || offer[i] == '*' {
					return offer
				}
				if accepted[i] != offer[i] {
					break
				}
			}
			if i == len(accepted) {
				return offer
			}
		}
	}
	return ""
}
```
Known bug: when an offered media type is SHORTER than the accepted one (e.g. Accept: "text/html" and offer "text"), `offer[i]` panics with "index out of range". A library must not panic here.

STEP 1 (probe, before editing): write a TEMPORARY test file /gin/zz_tmp_neg_test.go (package gin) that calls NegotiateFormat with many combinations and prints/asserts results, e.g. Accept header "text/html,application/xhtml+xml,application/xml;q=0.9;q=0.8" with offers: (MIMEJSON, MIMEXML), (MIMEXML, MIMEHTML), (MIMEJSON), ("text"), ("text/html"), ("application"), ("application/json; charset=utf-8"), (""); and Accept "*/*" and "text/*" variants. Record which calls PANIC and which return what. Report the full table of observed results (this is important evidence).

STEP 2 (fix): make the loop bounds-safe while preserving existing semantics:
```go
